OK, I'll chip in another $0.02...

Omega was notable for its scope, interesting ideas, plethora of bugs,
and utter lack of game balance.  Wild swings of fortune were common.
Pointless deaths, such as walking along, and falling into the infinite
abyss were the all-to frequent result.  The "shadowform" spell, along
with a couple of offensive spells made your character essentially
invincible....I could go on.

What was a damn shame was that the game had a lot of really neat ideas,
including the multiple dungeons, overland travel, horseback riding, and
the ability for a successful character to "become" one of the notables
in the game (such as the arena champion or justiciar).

What made me stop playing it was that all too soon, the game was not
challenging - I wouldn't necessarily win every game, but it seemed that
most deaths were of the pointless variety.  Actually most games ended
with a core dump.....

What keeps me playing nethack is that I can honestly say that most of
the time I have died, it has been my own fault.  Only once or twice
have I felt that I "played the p**s out" of a given game and still
not won.  I am amazed at what a balanced and challenging game it is.

Retaining the "balance" once you've gotten good at the game does mean
walking away from a few (arguable) misfeatures (such as not polypiling).
And you're right... hell is boring as, er Hell.  It's easy to criticize,
not so easy to come up with a better solution, but 

   [ Sound of gears shifting ]


OK.  Here's an idea I've been rolling around a bit.  What if the dungeon
stopped at level 36 ?  Furthermore, the Wiz would not be in it.  Instead,
you go down to level 36 and start working your way up.  Somewhere between
28-36 inclusive, will be Vlads tower.  You gotta find it, go in at the
base, and go upstairs to have an intimate chat with Vlad (who needs to
be less of a wimp).  After you and Vlad come to an accomodation, and
assuming that it's he that is in pieces on the floor, you must go over
to his throne and #sit... POOF!

The screen clears, you have been transported into a little room on the
left-hand edge of the screen.  You are sharing a square with another
throne, and there is an engraving as well that says "Sit not on this
throne without the Amulet of Yendor".  Yup, that's the way back to
Vlad's place, and it doesn't work without the amulet.  So you step
out of your little room to find a little room on the other side of
the screen, with the rest of the screen a giant battlefield.  On this
battlefield rages a pitched battle between lawful and chaotic monsters.
You have got to negotiate the battlefield.

Neutral characters will have it the easiest, since they'll only have
to worry about flying arrows, fire blasts, etc.  Lawful and chaotic
characters will be attacked by the other side as they try to cross.
The point here, is that the player should not become overly involved
in the battle.  New monsters will be constantly moving in from the
edge of the screen as others fall in battle.

Once the other side of the battlefield is reached, the player enters
the small room and finds the down staircase to Hell.  From here I
defer to some excellent suggestions made on the net last month,
that include a small road  (paved with "Good Intentions"), 
a 3-headed dog, etc.

Should the character make it into Hell, defeat the Wizard, and
come back with the amulet, the battlefield must be crossed in
the reverse direction, in order to get back to the "portal"
throne that will transport the player back to Vlad's tower.

OK - I've got my red dragon scale mail on.  Comments?


